data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"Imagine","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" you want to send a message to a friend across the globe. In milliseconds, your request","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" travels through a staggering, invisible web of global infrastructure. To understand how the internet works, it helps to look at it in","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" layers—from the physical ground beneath our feet up to the language your browser speaks.\n\n### 1. The Physical Layer","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":": Cables and Conduits\nAt its most fundamental level, the internet is just a massive network of connected computers.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" But how do those computers talk across oceans? \n\nDeep beneath the world’s oceans lie hundreds of fiber-optic cables.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" These hair-thin glass strands transmit data using pulses of light, traveling at nearly the speed of light. On land, data","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" moves through underground fiber-optic cables and copper telephone wires. \n\nIf you are using Wi-Fi, your device","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" isn't actually connected to the internet by a wire; instead, your laptop or phone uses radio waves to communicate with a local router.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" That router is physically plugged into a modem, which finally connects you to your Internet Service Provider’s (ISP) wired","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" network.\n\n### 2. Addresses and Delivery: IP and Packets\nData cannot travel all at once as a single massive","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" file. If you try to send a high-definition video, it gets chopped up into millions of tiny pieces called **packets**. Think","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of these like puzzle pieces, each stamped with an address.\n\nEvery device connected to the internet has a unique numerical label","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" called an **IP (Internet Protocol) address** (e.g., `192.168.1.1`).","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" When you request a webpage, your computer breaks your request into packets, each stamped with your computer's return IP address and the destination server","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"'s IP address. \n\n### 3. The Phonebook: DNS (Domain Name System)\nHumans are","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" bad at remembering long strings of numbers like `142.250.190.46`, but","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" we are great at names like `google.com`. This is where the **Domain Name System (DNS)** comes in. \n\nWhen you","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" type a URL into your browser, your computer queries a DNS server—essentially the internet’s phonebook. The DNS server looks up the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" human-friendly name and translates it into the correct IP address. Once your computer has the IP address, it knows where","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" to send those packets.\n\n### 4. Finding the Path: Routers and Routing\nOnce your packets enter the network","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", they don't take a straight, pre-determined line to their destination. Instead, they hop from one **","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"router** to another. \n\nRouters are the traffic cops of the internet. They look at the destination IP address on","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" an incoming packet and decide the fastest, most efficient path to send it next. Because internet traffic changes constantly, two packets belonging","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" to the same web page might take completely different routes around the world, only to be reassembled in the correct order when they arrive","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" at their destination.\n\n### 5. Keeping Order: TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)\nWith millions of packets flying","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" across various routes, some are bound to get lost, corrupted, or arrive out of order. This is the job of **TCP (","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"Transmission Control Protocol)**. \n\nTCP acts like a meticulous shipping manager. Before data is sent, TCP establishes a connection","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" between your computer and the server (a \"handshake\"). As packets arrive, TCP checks them for errors, asks for","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" missing or damaged packets to be resent, and carefully reassembles all the pieces in the exact right order before handing the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" data over to your application. (There is also UDP, a faster protocol used for live streaming or gaming where losing a packet","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" matters less than speed).\n\n### 6. The Language of the Web: HTTP/HTTPS\nWe have finally reached the top","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" layer: the software you actually see and interact with. Once the data packets are safely reassembled on your device, your web","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" browser needs to interpret them. This is done via **HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol)** or its secure cousin, **HTTPS**.\n\nHTTP","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" is the set of rules that governs how browsers and servers talk to each other. When you want a webpage, your browser sends an","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" HTTP \"GET\" request to the server. The server processes the request and sends back an HTTP response containing HTML code, images","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", scripts, and styling sheets. Your browser reads this code and renders it as the colorful, interactive webpage you see on your screen.\n\n###","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Summary\nThe internet is a marvel of cooperative engineering. In the blink of an eye, physical light pulses through ocean cables, packets","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of data are routed across continents based on numerical IP addresses, TCP ensures nothing is lost, DNS translates the names we type","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", and HTTP delivers the final webpage to your browser. It is a vast, decentralized symphony of technology working seamlessly together every single time you click","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

